---
import GrooveLayout from '../../../layouts/GrooveLayout.astro';
import Section from '../../../components/Section.astro';
import ModelBadge from '../../../components/groove/ModelBadge.astro';
import RankedBars from '../../../components/groove/RankedBars.astro';
import Figure from '../../../components/groove/Figure.astro';
import Note from '../../../components/groove/Note.astro';
import EmbeddingExplorer from '../../../components/groove/EmbeddingExplorer.astro';
import emb from '../../../data/groove/embedding-comparisons.json';
import { gpage } from '../../../lib/groove';

let n = 0;
const idx = () => String(++n).padStart(2, '0');
---

<GrooveLayout
  current="/representations"
  title="Representations"
  description="What the CNN and PaSST embeddings reveal: t-SNE structure, within model centroid shift stability, and why cross model distances aren't comparable."
  label="Representations"
  heading="What the learned embeddings reveal"
  lede="Two models can score similarly yet organize drum style very differently. Beyond macro-F1, the embeddings expose how each model structures style, and how stable that structure is."
>
  <Section index={idx()} label="Why look past accuracy">
    <p class="g-lead">
      Macro-F1 says <em>how often</em> a model is right, not <em>how</em> it understands the task. To
      compare understanding, the high dimensional embeddings of each model are projected to two dimensions
      with t-SNE and summarized by class <strong>centroids</strong>, the average position of each
      style. Tracking how far those centroids move between training runs measures the <strong
        >stability</strong
      > of a model's representation.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section
    index={idx()}
    label="Embedding structure · interactive"
    title="Explore the embedding space"
  >
    <p class="g-lead">
      Switch between models, then move the slider to reproject the same embeddings at different
      t-SNE perplexities. Low perplexity emphasises local neighbourhoods; high perplexity favours
      global structure. PaSST keeps coherent clusters by primary style across the whole sweep, while
      the CNN organises into smaller, more local groups.
    </p>
    <div class="g-gap">
      <EmbeddingExplorer />
    </div>
    <noscript>
      <div class="g-grid2">
        <Figure
          src="/figures/tsne-passt.webp"
          width={1372}
          height={989}
          alt="t-SNE of frozen PaSST embeddings, coloured by style."
          caption="PaSST embeddings (static snapshot)."
          source="PaSST_setup6_4.ipynb"
        />
        <Figure
          src="/figures/tsne-cnn.webp"
          width={1588}
          height={1474}
          alt="t-SNE of CNN embeddings, coloured by style."
          caption="CNN embeddings (static snapshot)."
          source="GMD_CNN_prototype5_3_tSNE.ipynb"
        />
      </div>
    </noscript>
  </Section>

  <Section
    index={idx()}
    label="Where the models confuse styles"
    title="Confusion concentrates on neighbouring styles"
  >
    <p class="g-lead">
      Both models keep a strong diagonal but stumble on stylistically adjacent classes, like jazz
      fast with dance breakbeat and jazz mediumfast, and funk with hip hop. The CNN also
      overpredicts the dominant <em>rock</em> classes, a symptom of class imbalance.
    </p>
    <div class="g-grid2">
      <Figure
        src="/figures/cnn-confusion.webp"
        width={1833}
        height={1589}
        alt="74 by 74 confusion matrix for the final CNN, dominated by a strong diagonal with sparse off diagonal confusions."
        caption="Final CNN (exp 10.1): confusion across all 74 classes."
        source="GMD_CNN_prototype6_4.ipynb"
      />
      <Figure
        src="/figures/passt-confusion.webp"
        width={1833}
        height={1589}
        alt="74 by 74 confusion matrix for the final PaSST model, with a strong diagonal and confusions among related styles."
        caption="Final PaSST (exp 11.2): confusion across all 74 classes."
        source="PaSST_setup6_16.ipynb"
      />
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section index={idx()} label="The caveat">
    <Note variant="warn" title="The most important caveat on this page">
      <p>
        The <ModelBadge model="cnn" /> and <ModelBadge model="passt" /> embeddings live in <strong
          >different coordinate systems with different distance scales</strong
        >. In absolute terms the CNN's centroids move about <strong>{emb.scaleRatio}×</strong> further
        than PaSST's (mean primary style shift {emb.cnn.meanPrimaryShift} vs {
          emb.passt.meanPrimaryShift
        }), but that ratio is an artifact of scale, <em>not</em> evidence that PaSST is “11× more stable.”
        The two charts below therefore use <strong>separate axes</strong> and are read only <em
          >within</em
        > each model.
      </p>
    </Note>
  </Section>

  <Section
    index={idx()}
    label="Stability under padding and augmentation"
    title="How far the centroids move, by intervention"
  >
    <p class="g-lead">
      Each bar is the mean distance a model's primary style centroids travelled from its baseline
      run under a given change. The scales are completely different: the CNN's space lurches under
      every intervention, reflection padding most of all, while PaSST barely moves. Read each model
      on its own axis.
    </p>
    <div class="g-grid2">
      <RankedBars
        title="CNN: centroid shift by intervention"
        model="cnn"
        items={emb.cnn.interventionShifts.map((d) => ({ label: d.label, value: d.shift }))}
        caption={`Mean primary style shift from the ${emb.cnn.interventionBaseline} baseline. Reflection padding moves it most.`}
        source="workbook · GMD_CNN_primary_embedding"
      />
      <RankedBars
        title="PaSST: centroid shift by intervention"
        model="passt"
        items={emb.passt.interventionShifts.map((d) => ({ label: d.label, value: d.shift }))}
        caption={`Mean primary style shift from the ${emb.passt.interventionBaseline} baseline. Note the axis: about ${emb.scaleRatio}× smaller than the CNN.`}
        source="workbook · PaSST_primary_embedding"
      />
    </div>

    <h3 class="sub-h">And which styles move the most</h3>
    <div class="g-grid2">
      <RankedBars
        title="CNN: most shifted primary styles"
        model="cnn"
        items={emb.cnn.topPrimary.map((p) => ({ label: p.style, value: p.shift }))}
        caption={`Under reflection padding, mean shift ${emb.cnn.meanPrimaryShift}`}
        source="workbook · GMD_CNN_primary_embedding"
      />
      <RankedBars
        title="PaSST: most shifted primary styles"
        model="passt"
        items={emb.passt.topPrimary.map((p) => ({ label: p.style, value: p.shift }))}
        caption={`Mean shift ${emb.passt.meanPrimaryShift}`}
        source="workbook · PaSST_primary_embedding"
      />
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section
    index={idx()}
    label="What it suggests"
    title="A more hierarchical space, at a cost in accuracy"
  >
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Taken together, PaSST's small, evenly distributed shifts and tight primary style clustering
        suggest its AudioSet pretraining carries a stable, hierarchical “world model” of audio that
        transfers to drum style. The CNN's large, uneven shifts, concentrated in a handful of
        styles, point to a more local, task fitted representation that is more sensitive to class
        imbalance and to interventions like padding.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is the heart of the thesis's nuance: the CNN wins on aggregate macro-F1, yet PaSST
        organizes style more robustly. Accuracy and representational quality are not the same axis.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="g-gap">
      <Note title="Read t-SNE with care">
        <p>
          t-SNE is an exploratory projection: distances and cluster sizes in 2D are not literal.
          These views motivate hypotheses about structure, not proof of semantic separation.
          Centroid shifts are reported within a model, as ranks and relative magnitudes.
        </p>
      </Note>
    </div>
    <p class="g-gap">
      <a class="g-more" href={gpage('/experiments')}>← Back to the experiments</a>
    </p>
  </Section>
</GrooveLayout>

<style>
  .sub-h {
    margin: var(--sp-7) 0 var(--sp-2);
    font-size: var(--step-1);
  }
</style>
